<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Eugene IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1383–<a href="/years/1447">1447</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Gabriele Condulmer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eugene IV, born Gabriele Condulmer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, was pope from 1431 to 1447. His reign was dominated by conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-basel">Council of Basel</a>, which deposed him and elected the antipope Felix V. He presided over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-florence">Council of Ferrara-Florence</a>, which proclaimed a short-lived union with the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> in 1439, and he spent years in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> after a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-eugene-iv.jpg" alt="Pope Eugene IV" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Domenico_di_Bartolo,_madonna_della_misericordia,_1444,_06(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Pope Eugene IV</b> completely reorganised the studium in 1431.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-20">20 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Eugene IV</b> presided over the first solemn session of the council he had moved from Basel to Ferrara, attended by the Eastern Roman Emperor and the Patriarch of Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-9">9 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Pietro Barbo, a Venetian and nephew of <b>Pope Eugene IV</b>, succeeded Pius II as pope, taking the name Paul II.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-30">30 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the bull Sicut dudum, issued at Florence, <b>Pope Eugene IV</b> forbade the enslavement of the Guanches of the Canary Islands who had converted, or were converting, to Christianity.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-13">13 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Meeting in the Roman basilica of Santa Maria sopra Minerva, the conclave elected Cardinal Tommaso Parentucelli to succeed <b>Pope Eugene IV</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-6">6 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1440 <b>Pope Eugene IV</b> sold Borgo Sansepolcro to the Republic of Florence.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-cospaia">Republic of Cospaia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1424" aria-label="Venice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4843 561 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Eugene IV"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5293 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5293 831h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1424">What was here in 1424? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
